# <%= it.scope %>/i18n

Translations for every app here: everything in [`@mfeflow/i18n`](https://www.npmjs.com/package/@mfeflow/i18n),
plus the company's languages and namespaces and two helpers that apply them. `/react` is re-exported too.
[`<%= it.scope %>/storage`](../storage) is installed with it: the chosen language is kept in storage, so the two
are released together. `i18next` and `react-i18next` are peer dependencies; the app that installs this package
installs them.

## What to fill in

`src/defaults.ts` holds the settings:

- `languages`: the languages the apps are translated into, as `{ code, label }`. English and Spanish are
  placeholders.
- `defaultLanguage`: the one to fall back to. It must be in `languages`, or `startI18n` rejects.
- `namespaces`: the names an app may load: `common` for shared text, then one per remote.

`i18nOptions(overrides?)` returns the options for `initI18n`, and `startI18n(overrides?)` calls it. A remote loads
its own messages with `loadCompanyNamespace(instance, 'catalog', loader)`, which is `loadNamespace` with the
namespace checked against the list.

In the shell:

```ts
import { startI18n } from '<%= it.scope %>/i18n';

const i18n = await startI18n(); // or startI18n({ detect: false })
```

## Settings only, no state

`@mfeflow/i18n` is a Module Federation singleton, shared by package name. `<%= it.scope %>/i18n` is not in that
list, so every remote gets its own copy of this package while the one underneath stays shared. That is fine while
this package holds only settings and functions that build things. Keep it that way: no instance, language or flag
at module level here. State belongs in `@mfeflow/i18n` or in the shell.

## In an app

An app that imports `<%= it.scope %>/i18n` must also list `@mfeflow/i18n` in its own `package.json` (the shell and every
remote). The preset shares `@mfeflow/i18n` only when the app can resolve it from its own folder, and pnpm does not
make a dependency of a dependency visible there. Without it the preset cannot see the package, leaves it out of the shared list, and each remote gets its own copy, so a remote does not see the language the shell set.
